            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The valley primarily symbolizes a difficult season of hardship, trial, suffering, and spiritual darkness, with secondary associations including judgment, humility, and restoration.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The valley symbolizes a transitional path of descent, humility, service, and obedience unto death between mountain-kingdoms, leading to resurrection and exaltation.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A season or place of trial, affliction, and spiritual low points.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The humbled passage of descent, service, and obedience between mountain-kingdoms.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the common application of valleys to generic 'low seasons of trial' or passive suffering, instead applying the symbol to deliberate acts of humbling, service, and obedience, mapping it to Christ's kenotic descent.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus defines the valley primarily as a negative life experience (hardship or suffering) where God provides comfort, whereas the book defines it as a deliberate posture and path of descent and obedience by which one transitions between authorities.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly contrasts its definition with the common view ('low seasons of trial'), replacing the concept of passive hardship with an active theological framework of descent, service, and transitioning between kingdoms.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Philippians 2 does not use the word 'valley,' meaning the book's core theological definition relies on a conceptual mapping of descent and ascent rather than explicit biblical usage of the symbol itself. Furthermore, framing a valley as a passage 'between mountain-kingdoms' imposes a highly specific paradigm onto general topographical language.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
